{
  "slug": "kth-largest-element-in-an-array",
  "title": "Kth Largest Element in an Array",
  "topic": "heap",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "heap / Quickselect",
  "statement": "# Kth Largest Element in an Array\n\nAn unsorted integer array `nums` and an integer `k` are given. Order the values from largest to smallest; the element in position `k` (1-based) is the kth largest, counting duplicates separately rather than collapsing them.\n\nFor `k = 1` this is the maximum of the array, and for `k = nums.length` it is the minimum.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 50`, `-100 <= nums[i] <= 100`, and `1 <= k <= nums.length`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 4], k = 2",
      "output": "5",
      "explanation": "Sorted descending the array reads [6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]; the element in position 2 is 5."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [3, 2, 3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 5, 6], k = 4",
      "output": "4",
      "explanation": "Sorted descending: [6, 5, 5, 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 1]; duplicates count separately, so position 4 holds 4."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [7, 7, 7, 7], k = 1",
      "output": "7",
      "explanation": "Every element is 7, so the maximum (k = 1) is trivially 7."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function findKthLargest(nums: number[], k: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Sorting answers the query; ask which ranked index the kth largest occupies.",
    "A partition around a pivot tells you exactly which ranks fall left and right of it.",
    "Recurse only into the side containing rank n - k and stop when the pivot settles there."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The kth largest is the element at sorted index n - k. Quickselect partitions around a pivot and recurses only into the side holding that index, using a three-way split so duplicate values settle together.",
    "why_optimal": "Partitioning discards a fraction of the array per round for linear average time with constant extra space, instead of sorting everything. On 44 generated cases PAF measured the brute force at 0.19 ms and the optimal at 0.24 ms.",
    "pitfalls": "Translate k to the ascending index n - k before partitioning. A two-way partition degrades on heavy duplicates; the three-way split keeps equal values out of further rounds."
  },
